| `16.2-integral-domains-and-fields.md` | 16 — Rings, §16.2 Integral Domains and Fields | ✅ the Cancellation Law BOTH ways, **Wedderburn's theorem** (every finite integral domain is a field) following Judson with our size mechanism substituted for his one-sentence appeal to finiteness — $D^\ast$ as a `Set`, $\lambda_a$ as an `Fn`, and "since $D^\ast$ is finite the map must be onto" discharged by `std/set/self-map.b4m`. Characteristic defined; Lemma 16.6 and Theorem 16.7 proved from Judson's displayed identities, which are hypotheses (repeated addition past a product is an induction `std/group/power.b4m` does not carry). |
| `16.3-ring-homomorphisms-and-ideals.md` | 16 — Rings, §16.3 Ring Homomorphisms and Ideals | ✅ ring homomorphisms over TWO instances of the ring theory, $\phi(0) = 0$, ideals (subring conditions + Judson's two absorption conditions), an ideal containing 1 is everything, the principal ideal $\langle a \rangle$ verified ideal-by-ideal as he does, **the kernel is an ideal** (both displayed computations), and the $R/I$ product WELL-DEFINED on cosets — the precondition for the factor ring. Left in prose: 16.12–16.16, which need $R/I$ as a sort and name a $\cong$ between rings (§11.2's limit). |
| `16.4-maximal-and-prime-ideals.md` | 16 — Rings, §16.4 Maximal and Prime Ideals | ✅ maximal ideals (the arbitrary larger ideal as a second membership predicate, the `std/subgroup.b4m` idiom), **Proposition 16.18 BOTH ways** ($P$ prime iff $R/P$ an integral domain, through `std/ring/factor.b4m`'s kernel theorem), the forward half of Theorem 16.17 from the point his construction $I = \{ra+m\}$ delivers $1 = ab+m$, its converse in full, and Corollary 16.19. Noether's historical note transliterated. Left aside: building $I$ from an element needs comprehension over a non-`Element` sort. |
| `17.1-polynomial-rings.md` | 17 — Polynomials, §17.1 Polynomial Rings | ✅ $R[x]$ AS A SORT: a polynomial is its coefficient family (`coeff(p, i)` reified, the `std/indexed.b4m` idiom), equality-by-coefficients as extensionality, and the convolution $\sum_{k\le i} a_k b_{i-k}$ as a fold. **Theorem 17.1 less his associativity display**: the abelian group in full (each $R$'s own axiom coefficientwise), $1$ as the identity via an induction collapsing the convolution, and **BOTH halves of his closing exercise** — distributivity, and commutativity through the REINDEXING lemma (`convolutionIsReversible` in `std/ring/polynomial.b4m`, a two-bound segment grown at the top and peeled at the base; three other induction shapes fail, see `drafts/convolution-reindexing/`). **Proposition 17.4's** leading-product step (where the integral-domain hypothesis does its work) and **Theorem 17.5's** additive half plus $\phi_\alpha(1) = 1$. Judson's chapter-wide "commutative ring with identity" is stated as two axioms, since `std/ring.b4m` is the bare ring. Left in prose: his associativity flattens a NESTED double sum into $j+k+l=i$, a different lemma from the single-sum reversal with no primitive-recursive shape in one bound; $\phi_\alpha$'s multiplicative half is that same identity; $(R[x])[y]$ needs the construction over its own output. |
| `17.2-the-division-algorithm.md` | 17 — Polynomials, §17.2 The Division Algorithm | ✅ **Theorem 17.6 (Division Algorithm)**, both halves, with each of his four MOVES written out as its own theorem: the zero-polynomial case, the low-degree case ($q=0$, $r=f$), the quotient extension ($q = q' + \frac{a_n}{b_m}x^{n-m}$, proved from recovery + commutativity + distributivity), and the uniqueness pivot (a nonzero multiple of $g$ has degree $\geq \deg g$, so a lower-degree multiple is the zero multiple). **Corollary 17.7 (the factor theorem)** BOTH directions: forward via his $0 = p(\alpha) = 0 \cdot q(\alpha) + a = a$, converse via his $0 \cdot q(\alpha)$. Two asides: his $n-m$ becomes a gap WITNESS (truncated subtraction lies when $m>n$) and his induction on $n$ becomes strong induction on a degree BOUND ($f'$ is not $f$'s structural predecessor). Deferred with reasons: **Corollary 17.8** (at most $n$ zeros) needs counting over a field's carrier, which `std` has only over `Element`; **Proposition 17.10** (gcd) needs a least element of a SET of polynomials — the §16.4 comprehension limit. |
| `*-exercises.md` | — the segregated chapter exercises | ✅ each Judson end-of-chapter exercise is proved (`std` used liberally), forwarded to a main-text proof, skipped (a brute numeric computation), or **deferred** with a prose note naming the missing machinery. The master deferred index, grouped by machinery, is `EXERCISES-DEFERRED`. |

The set theory rests on `std/set.b4m` (sets over an abstract `Universe` of
elements, fixed membershipwise, with extensionality). Every identity is proved
by the same recipe — extensionality → fix an element → unfold `member(x, ·)` →
one `[using tautology …]` — so every step in the chapter is kernel-checked.

The function theory rests on `std/function.b4m` (maps as opaque `Fn` objects
over a `Universe`, with `apply`/`compose`, an identity, and
injective/surjective/invertible, fixed by their axioms plus **function
extensionality**). Composition associativity is a funcExt rewrite chain; the
preservation and invertibility results are FOL over the defining predicates,
with existential witnesses (`g(b)` for onto, the unpacked inverse for
invertible⇒bijective).

The group theory rests on `std/group.b4m` (the group axioms as a reusable 2b4m
theory) and uses the `assoc` tactic (associativity-only reordering) for the
non-commutative rearrangements.

## What's deferred (the forcing-function backlog)

Most of the textbook needs machinery 2b4m does not yet have. Rather than skip
those results silently, each is kept in the relevant `.md` with an in-place note
(`**Deferred:** …` for an exercise, or a `// this section cannot currently be
encoded [reason]` block for a stated theorem) saying exactly what it would
require — a running backlog of standard-library constructions that future
chapters will motivate:

- ~~a concrete **ℤ_n / U(n)** model (modular arithmetic as a group sort)~~ — **the
  ALGEBRA now LANDED** in `std/integer-mod-n.b4m`: ℤ_n as a quotient sort whose
  group/ring axioms lift from ℤ (`ZnGroup`/`ZnGroupPower`/`ZnRing` models, ℤ_n = ⟨1⟩
  cyclic), and U(n) as the units group (`UnitsGroup`). An abstract template modeled at
  a concrete n (e.g. `scratch-needswork/z6.b4m`). What remains for the ℤ_n exercises is
  the FINITE/counting content — the generator COUNT φ(n) — not the algebra; "ℤ_n has
  exactly n elements" LANDED 2026-09-27 as a LISTING (`std/integer/mod-n-listing.b4m`),
  which also carries §4.1 Ex 29 by model transfer,
- **finite sets + cardinality** (counting arguments, Cayley tables, Lagrange,
  Euler's totient φ; "ℤ_n has n elements" / "U(n) is cyclic" as finite facts),
- **matrix / GLₙ** constructions and the reals,
- **sets-of-sets / quotient structure + a relation sort** — Chapter 1's §1.2.3
  (equivalence relations ⟺ partitions) needs equivalence classes `[a]` and the
  partition `X = ⋃[x]`, which `std/set.b4m`'s flat `member : Universe → Set`
  model cannot express; and the **indexed families** `⋃ᵢ Aᵢ` / `⋂ᵢ Aᵢ` need a
  sort of finite sequences of `Set`,
- a **choice / inverse-construction operator** — Chapter 1's §1.2.2 backward
  direction (bijective ⇒ invertible) builds the inverse by "letting `g(b) = a`"
  for the unique preimage `a`; that definite description (turning "for each `b`
  a unique `a`" into a function value) is not expressible in 2b4m's first-order
  logic, so `invertibleDef`'s `exists g` cannot be discharged,
- a **pair sort `Universe × Universe`** (with a set-of-pairs theory) for the
  §1.2.2 Cartesian products `A × B` and the pairs-based definition of a
  function; `std/function.b4m` sidesteps it by taking `apply(f, x)` primitive.

Each is a self-contained addition; the translation grows as they land.
